In this lesson, we'll look at how to use a sample to make a smart guess about a whole population. Once you've collected your sample data, the summary statistics you calculate become your best estimates of the true population values. So the three main point estimates are these. The sample mean estimates the population mean. The sample median estimates the population median. And the sample proportion estimates the population proportion. Now, a few things to keep in mind for confidence and caution. First, bigger samples give you more precise estimates because there's less random variation to throw you off. Second, watch out for bias. If your sampling method is biased, your estimate will be wrong on average, and no amount of extra data can fix that. And third, remember that any single estimate is just one number, so a confidence interval is really useful for showing the uncertainty around it. Now let's see it in action.